======================== Load Balancer ========================
Load Balancer
Distributes traffic
It does NOT understand business intent.

Example:
Real Users -> 10K requests
Bots -> 50K requests

A normal load balancer may still distribute both equally across servers.

Modern L7 load balancers like Amazon Web Services ALB support:
path-based routing
header-based routing
limited filtering

Example:
/orders  -> Order Service
/payments -> Payment Service
/users -> User Service

This slightly narrows the gap between load balancers and API gateways.

======================== Internal Load Balancer ========================
Used for:
East-West traffic
traffic between internal services.

Example:
Frontend -> Order Service
Order Service -> Payment Service
Benefits
Internal traffic distribution
Service scaling
High availability
Independent scaling of services
Better resource utilization

Example:
Scale Order Service only
without scaling everything else.

======================== API Gateway ========================
Governs traffic
while load balancer mainly:
Moves traffic

API Gateway Responsibilities
1. Authentication
OAuth2
API Keys
JWT validation
identity verification

2. Rate Limiting / Throttling
Prevents:
abuse
bot traffic
accidental overload
Example:
100 requests/minute per user

3. Routing
path-based routing
service targeting
version routing

Example:
/v1/users (old users)
/v2/users (new users)

4. Policies & Governance
input validation
request transformation
security rules
quota management
Edge Protection

API gateways often sit behind:
WAF
CDN
DDoS protection

They act as:
First security shield
against malicious traffic.

======================== When to Use Only Load Balancer ========================

Good enough for:
internal traffic only
simple architectures
early-stage startups
limited public APIs

======================== When API Gateway is Required ========================
Use API Gateway when you need:

public APIs
authentication
rate limiting
partner integrations
centralized governance
API versioning
======================== Do NOT Use API Gateway Internally Everywhere ========================

Problems:
extra latency
operational overhead
unnecessary centralization

Better options:
service mesh
internal load balancing

======================== API Gateway Drawbacks ========================

If poorly designed:
Gateway becomes single point of failure

Needs:
replicas
clustering
monitoring
failover

Typical HA setup:
Clients
-> External Load Balancer
-> Multiple API Gateway Replicas
-> Backend Services

Monitoring stack commonly includes:
Prometheus
Grafana
Elasticsearch
Jaeger

======================== Managed vs Self-Hosted API Gateways ========================

Managed gateways:
easy initially
low operational effort

Example:
Amazon API Gateway

Problem:
Costs become huge at scale

Self-hosted alternatives:
Kong
NGINX

Reasons for self-hosting:
lower cost
full control
custom policies
performance tuning